  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "This team doesn't fold when it matters. They chase late. Hold in the seventh, stay alive in the sixth. And that margin matters. Merrill kept them within arm's reach while France delivered the actual turn, but the real story is simpler: they were down four and they answered. Not with early brilliance or calculated depth. With conviction when it counted. Ty France was the swing that mattered, sure. But Jackson Merrill is why they were still standing when France got his chance. Two home runs. Two RBI each. That's not luck. That's the offense doing its actual job when the game narrows.",
    "what_this_means": "Griffin Canning held the middle innings scoreless over 3.1. That bridge matters. But here's what matters more: the Padres scored five runs with runners in scoring position, converted what was there to convert, and did it late. They were chasing the whole way until they weren't. That's the arc. Six runners left on base isn't a story when five of them scored runs first. The pattern here is late-inning rallies becoming their baseline survival tool. And it's holding because the role players keep showing up. France, Merrill, the bullpen arms stretching the game into the seventh and beyond. Two straight wins by one run each says something about how thin the margins are. But they're not folding.",
    "what_to_watch": "Bailey Ober takes the mound for Minnesota tonight with a 4.62 ERA. Walker Buehler (3.13 over his last four starts) gets the ball for the Padres. If the bullpen holds and the order makes something happen late, this becomes three straight.",
